Republican lawmakers are dismissing the  chances of a government shutdown this  October one month before the midterm  elections now that is despite president  Trump's tweet that he quote would be  willing to shut down the government if  the Democrats do not give us the votes  for border security which includes the  wall Nancy Cordes is on Capitol Hill  Nancy good morning good morning and that  tweet came as a surprise to Republican  leaders who thought they had conveyed to  the president how difficult it would be  for them to secure billions of dollars  in wall funding by October they warned  that a shutdown and immigration fight  one month before the midterm elections  could cost the party time and votes I  certainly don't like playing a shutdown  politics Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson  was one of several Republicans this  weekend actively discouraging the White  House from courting a shutdown I don't  think it'd be helpful so let's try and  avoid it the shutdown threat was a  presidential attempt at hardball over  his long sought funding for a border  wall but the Senate's Republican leader  Mitch McConnell said Congress may not be  able to get to that until December at  the earliest is the funding of the  border wall going to wait until after  the midterm elections probably and  that's something we do have a  disagreement on McConnell and House  Speaker Paul Ryan met with the president  last week to discuss the fall  legislative agenda and the obstacles to  quick funding of the wall the  president's willing to be patient to  make sure that we get what we need so  that we can get that done because border  security is extremely important but the  president's patience now appears to be  wearing thin though he has backed off  similar threats before if we don't  change it let's have a shutdown will do  a shutdown and it's worth it for our  country the yeas are 71 that time there  was a shutdown but it lasted just a  couple hours and with the midterm  elections looming Republicans are hoping  to avoid a shutdown of any length we're  gonna make sure we keep the government  open but we're gonna get better policies  on immigration the president has already  secured about 1.5 billion dollars in  border security but he wants up to 25  billion to build the wall and Democrats  have signaled that they're open to that  but only if  publicans agree to protect the daka  population those so-called dreamers and  so far anthony congress has been unable  to find a compromise and now the house  is gone for the next five weeks Nancy  Cordes Thank You Nancy
 

